Because the scalar and pseudoscalar Wilson coefficients are still negligible, the **only place where \(\Delta C_{10}\) appears** is in the overall amplitude | If \(\Delta C_{10}\) is taken to be **real** (the most common benchmark) the phase of the amplitude does **not** change, so  

\[
A_{\Delta\Gamma}=  rac{{m Re}\! igl[(C_{10})^{2} igr]}
{|C_{10}|^{2}} =  rac{(C_{10}^{m SM}+\Delta C_{10})^{2}}{(C_{10}^{m SM}+\Delta C_{10})^{2}} = +1 .\]

Hence, **a real shift of \(\Delta C_{10}\) leaves \(A_{\Delta\Gamma}\) unchanged at +1**. | For a **real** NP shift \(\Delta C_{10}\) (|ΔC10|\,\ll\,|C10|) :

\[ oxed{
 rac{\delta\overline{\mathcal B}}{\overline{\mathcal B}}
\simeq
2\, rac{\Delta C_{10}}{C_{10}^{m SM}}
\;\;\;( 	ext{≈ } \pm 0.047 	ext{ for } \Delta C_{10}= \pm0.1 )
}
\]

In words: when two neutron stars (NSs) coalesce, the immediate post‑merger remnant is **either**

1. a **hyper‑massive neutron star (HMNS)** – a transient, differentially rotating object whose mass exceeds the maximum mass that can be supported by a uniformly rotating NS;  
2. a **prompt black‑hole (BH)** – a BH that forms essentially at the moment of contact (within a few ms). | Therefore, the proposition stands robust, provided its scope is understood to refer to the **initial post‑merger object** (the first milliseconds) rather than the *long‑term* fate of the remnant. | All of these lines of evidence converge on the picture that **the immediate post‑merger product falls into one of two categories** (HMNS or prompt BH).

The rare‑decay bound derived in the previous answer is

\[
\frac{|g_{bL}\,g_{\mu L}|}{M_{Z'}^2}
\;\lesssim\;3\times10^{-10}\;{\rm GeV}^{-2}.
\tag{1}
\] | Insert the ansatz:

